The gradient of the log is (gradient of the policy / the policy)
    gradient of the policy corresponds to the amount of exploitation
    dividing by the policy is like dividing by the expectation

Advantages: The main idea is that when you adjust your returns, in terms of the actions you pick, it wont affect your actual action taken (Deterministic towards action with highest return)
    - Defined as (Q values) - normal values; the Q values can sometimes be replaced by the returns, then becoms similar to TD and monte carlo methods
    - A TD error is a unbiased sample of the advantage for one step


Value Based Network: methods focus on learning the value of states and actions to guide decision-making
    - takes as input current state and current policy
        - One way to input the policy would be by conditioning the NN
        - Another way would be to just use a bootstrapped version of the policy (not quite as good but can be better with excessively large inputs)
    - outputs a prediction of the values of that state

Think of all NN as function approximators

Backwards view is when you are dealing with TD targets, decay eventually becomes 0; TD targets can be thought of as a discrepency in rewards
Forwards view is when you are dealing with TD rewards, constant decay

Actor Critic:
    - In short, means we will determine values based on a NN
    - Over a dozen modern RL models are considered actor critic
    - important that the critic is more well learned that the actor (This allows for the actor to get good learning)
